{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, add a jointly trained residual feed-forward block to GRU outputs before the unchanged recency readout: h'=h+W2(GELU(W1(h))), with hidden width 128, biases zero and W2 initialized to zero. This preserves the initial mapping while allowing nonlinear refinement within each category's single recurrent model. Keep embeddings, readout settings, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer fixed; update the predictor writer for matching checkpoint parameters.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, increase the existing causal recency-pooling mixture from 0.25 to 0.40 and its half-life from 3 to 4 observed interactions. The previous pooling improvement motivates testing slightly broader recent-interest retention while keeping substantial weight on the current GRU state. Apply identical causal pooling during training and inference, mask padding, retain final-real-position selection, and save both settings in predictor metadata. Keep other components fixed.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, increase the pointwise logistic coefficient from 0.2 to 0.4: softplus(negative-positive)+0.4*[softplus(-positive)+softplus(negative)], with the existing nonpadding reduction. Reducing this coefficient to 0.05 regressed on a related input; stronger absolute-score supervision may stabilize the current untied normalized embeddings. Change only the loss coefficient, preserving sampling, scoring, GRU architecture, readout, optimizer and training settings.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, test a bounded causal popularity-change input feature: scale each existing history embedding by 1+0.1*tanh(log1p(count(previous_item))-log1p(count(current_item))), using category training counts only. Give the first real item scale 1 and keep padding zero. Relative popularity changes may signal interest shifts more effectively than the previously tested absolute-frequency scaling. Preserve embedding parameters, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer; checkpoint counts and reproduce the feature in inference."
}
